 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Low IoU:** Predicted masks with IoU $\le 0.05$ are treated as unlabeled areas and do not participate in training [S1].
-*   **Occlusion:** Partially occluded objects may lead to missing or erroneous mask predictions in different views, which can mislead the model if not handled by refinement [S1].
-*   **Background:** Masks belonging to background must be filtered out using the class mapping [S1].
+- **Partial Occlusion:** Uniformly sampled images often contain partially occluded objects, which can mislead the pre-trained Mask2Former model, leading to missing or erroneous masks [S1].
+- **Background Noise:** Background masks must be explicitly filtered using the class mapping; otherwise, they may incorrectly match to 3D instances [S1].
+- **No Match:** If a predicted mask does not exceed the IoU threshold with any 3D instance, it is discarded. This prevents noise from corrupting the instance field [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the Mask2Former inference ($O(N \cdot T_{seg})$) and the projection/IoU computation ($O(K \cdot N \cdot P)$, where $K$ is the number of 3D instances, $N$ is the number of views, and $P$ is the number of pixels).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing 3D instance masks and 2D projected masks for all views.
-*   **Strengths:** Ensures multi-view consistency for instance fields, which is crucial for quality [S1]. Decouples segmentation from NeRF training initially.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the initial 3D instance detection and the pre-trained segmentation model. May fail on objects with poor visibility or severe occlusion without refinement [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When training instance-aware NeRF models (e.g., Instance-NeRF) and multi-view consistency of 2D masks is required.
-*   **When not to use:** When only single-view supervision is available or when 3D instance detection is not feasible.
+- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the initial Mask2Former inference and the projection/IoU computation. The number of comparisons is $O(N_{views} \times N_{pred\_masks} \times N_{3d\_instances})$.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing 3D instance masks and 2D predicted masks for all views.
+- **Strengths:** Ensures multi-view consistency for instance fields, which is critical for high-quality instance decomposition [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Relies heavily on the quality of the initial 3D instance detection (NeRF-RCNN) and the 2D segmentation model (Mask2Former). Errors in these models propagate to the instance field [S1].
+- **When to use:** When training instance-specific NeRF fields (Instance-NeRF) where consistent labeling across views is required [S1].
+- **When not to use:** For standard view synthesis without instance-level supervision, or when 3D instance detection is unavailable [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the context of Instance Neural Radiance Field (Instance-NeRF) to address the inconsistency of 2D instance masks across different views [S1]. It builds on NeRF-RCNN for 3D instance detection and Mask2Former for 2D segmentation [S1]. The approach is part of a pipeline that includes 2D mask refinement to handle occlusions and partial visibility [S1].
+The method was introduced in "Instance Neural Radiance Field" (Instance-NeRF) [S1]. It addresses the inconsistency of 2D instance masks across different views, which makes direct use in instance field training difficult [S1]. The approach builds on NeRF-RCNN for 3D instance detection and Mask2Former for 2D segmentation [S1].
 
